+## 3. Plan A — SHA-pin all 27 third-party refs (mechanical, ~27 edits)
+
+Rewrite each `uses:` as a full-length commit SHA plus a version comment so humans can audit future bumps (never-do: SHA-pin without version comment):
+
+```
+uses: actions/checkout@fbc6f3992d24b796d5a048ff273f7fcc4a7b6c09 # v5
+uses: actions/setup-python@ece7cb06caefa5fff74198d8649806c4678c61a1 # v6
+uses: actions/setup-node@a0853c24544627f65ddf259abe73b1d18a591444 # v5
+uses: pnpm/action-setup@f40ffcd9367d9f12939873eb1018b921a783ffaa # v4
+uses: actions/upload-artifact@b7c566a772e6b6bfb58ed0dc250532a479d7789f # v6
+```
+
+Fastest safe application: a repo-wide textual substitution per pair (tag form to SHA form), then `git grep -c "uses: .*@[0-9a-f]\{40\}" .github` must report 27. Once dependabot's `github-actions` ecosystem lands (#1736), SHA bumps become automatic PRs; until then re-run the ls-remote check monthly or on tag-retarget alerts.
+
+## 4. Plan B — de-injection of the 13 template-injection findings
+
+All sites are `run:` blocks interpolating `github.*` context directly into shell. Two patterns cover every finding.
+
+### 4.1 duplicate-pr-guard, pipeline.yml L311-L367 (head_ref into grep pipeline)
+
+The env block (L312-L317) already does the right thing for PR_TITLE/PR_BODY/PR_LABELS. The one true injection is L325: `github.head_ref` is interpolated inside double quotes directly on the shell command line. Attacker-controlled branch name executes in a privileged context on every external PR.
+
+Edit 1 — extend the step's existing env block:
+
+```
+        env:
+          GH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
+          PR_NUM: ${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}
+          PR_TITLE: ${{ github.event.pull_request.title }}
+          PR_BODY: ${{ github.event.pull_request.body }}
+          PR_LABELS: ${{ join(github.event.pull_request.labels.*.name, ' ') }}
+          HEAD_REF: ${{ github.head_ref }}
+```
+
+Edit 2 — inside the run block, replace the double-quoted template interpolation of `github.head_ref` with the env variable (same grep pipeline, attacker data now arrives as data, never as code):
+
+```
+          BRANCH_ISSUES=$(echo "$HEAD_REF" | grep -oE 'issue-?[0-9]+' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' | sed 's/^/#/' || true)
+```
+
+### 4.2 Watchdog curl sites (6 steps, first interpolation rows at L610, L642, L679, L731, L844, L926)
+
+Each builds a JSON payload for repository dispatch with `${{ github.ref_name }}`, `${{ github.repository }}`, `${{ github.workflow }}`, `${{ github.run_id }}`, `${{ github.sha }}` spliced into the curl argument string (canonical live specimen: secrets-scan "Trigger Federation CI Watcher on Failure" step). On pull_request events `github.ref_name` is the attacker-named head branch; JSON metacharacters in it break out of the payload.
+
+Fix pattern per site — move the values into the step's env, build the JSON with jq from argv (never string-concatenated), and hand curl a file:
+
+```
+        env:
+          WF_REPO: ${{ github.repository }}
+          WF_NAME: ${{ github.workflow }}
+          WF_RUN_ID: ${{ github.run_id }}
+          WF_REF: ${{ github.ref_name }}
+          WF_SHA: ${{ github.sha }}
+          DISPATCH_TOKEN: ${{ secrets.OPEN_SYNC_PAT || secrets.MERGE_PAT || secrets.OPEN_DISPATCH_TOKEN || secrets.GITHUB_TOKEN }}
+        run: |
+          jq -n --arg repo "$WF_REPO" --arg name "$WF_NAME" \
+                --argjson run "$WF_RUN_ID" --arg ref "$WF_REF" --arg sha "$WF_SHA" \
+                '{event_type: "federation_ci_failed",
+                  client_payload: {repo: $repo, workflow_name: $name, run_id: $run,
+                                   branch: $ref, commit_sha: $sha,
+                                   html_url: ("https://github.com/" + $repo + "/actions/runs/" + ($run | tostring))}}' \
+             > /tmp/payload.json
+          curl -s -X POST -H "Accept: application/vnd.github+json" \
+               -H "Authorization: Bearer ${DISPATCH_TOKEN}" \
+               https://api.github.com/repos/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open/dispatches \
+               --data-binary @/tmp/payload.json || true
+```
+
+Notes for the applying admin: keep each site's existing event_type and payload keys identical (downstream watchers parse them); `--argjson run` assumes run_id is numeric (it is); keep the trailing `|| true` failure-tolerance semantics unchanged.
+
+## 5. Plan C — permissions: 3 findings are the top-level write block
+
+zizmor flags the three write scopes of the top-level `permissions:` block at L68-L72 (contents: write, pull-requests: write, issues: write), inherited by all 21 jobs. Fix: set the top-level block to empty and grant per job, least privilege:
+
+- Read-only default: top-level `permissions: {}` then `permissions: {contents: read}` (or `contents: read` plus what the job needs) on every job.
+- Jobs that genuinely need `contents: write` (auto-landing, satellite-dispatch-and-sync push paths, any autofix commit step) declare it explicitly at job level.
+- Jobs posting comments/labels (the guards) declare `issues: write` and/or `pull-requests: write` at job level.
+- The duplicate-pr-guard only reads: `pull-requests: read` + `issues: read` suffice; note its env already uses GH_TOKEN for search API reads.
+
+This is the only plan item with per-job judgment; expect ~21 small edits. Suggested order: apply Plans A/B first (pure text), then permissions, re-running the gate after each batch.
+
+## 6. Plan D — artipacked: 15 findings (checkout without persist-credentials: false)
+
+All 15 `actions/checkout@v5` steps currently persist the GITHUB_TOKEN into the workspace `.git`. Audit one-liner for the applying admin: list checkout steps whose job has no push/commit step downstream; add `with: persist-credentials: false` to those. Steps whose job later pushes to the repo (auto-landing, satellite-dispatch-and-sync, any autofix) must keep credentials or switch to a explicit token-bearing remote URL — flag those in review rather than blind-applying. Expect 10-13 of the 15 to be safely de-credentialed.
+
+## 7. Plan E — self-repository: 2 findings, review note (no mechanical fix)
+
+Both findings are the second step of `secrets-scan` and `mcp-tower` referencing first-party local composites (`uses: ./.github/actions/gitleaks-scan`, `uses: ./.github/actions/setup-node-tower`). zizmor's point: on pull_request events the composite content comes from the PR head, i.e. PR-author-controlled code running with repo secrets in reach. The repo already treats these composites as first-party review surfaces; the documented mitigations are (a) the existing competing-PR guard, (b) requiring maintainer approval for workflows changed — which GitHub enforces for first-time contributors — and (c) keeping secrets out of jobs that run on untrusted head refs. Verdict: accept-with-justification is reasonable; document it in the workflow comment when applying Plans A-D. No edit required by this runbook.
+
+## 8. Verification recipe (after applying)
+
+1. Re-run the scan offline (same command as Section 1). Expected delta: unpinned-uses 27 to 0; template-injection 13 to 0; excessive-permissions 3 to 0; artipacked 15 to the count of push-needing checkouts retained; self-repository 2 unchanged (accepted, Section 7).
+2. Push-plane sanity: the pipeline's own push CI must stay green on a non-protected branch (per #2125, push-CI darkness let a red land before — do not trust schedule-plane only).
+3. Optional gate: add zizmor as a report-only CI step (action or pip CLI) with a shrink-only findings baseline, mirroring the repo's existing guard pattern (bandit/dep-CVE style) so posture can only improve.
+
+## 9. never-do (lane discipline)
+
+- Agents never edit `.github/**` (#1643); this whole plan is admin-owned.
+- Every SHA-pin carries a version comment (auditability of future bumps).
+- No secret values echoed during triage; token env refs stay as-is.
+- No auto-remediation bot; apply Plans A/B, then C, then D in separate commits so any regression is bisectable.
+- Report-only until admin flips the gate.
